This induction concerns cohomology alone, and the Orlik–Solomon algebra plays no part in it.
Say that $`\mathcal{A}` is _generated_ if $`C(U_{\mathcal{A}})` is spanned over $`B` by finite products of the classes $`[f_H]`, and _bounded_ if its homogeneous pieces vanish outside $`0\le p\le\dim V`.
These are predicates to be proved, and they are not assumptions on the theory.
The induction is on $`|\mathcal{A}|`, over all finite-dimensional ambient spaces.

# Why the Gysin map vanishes

The axioms give a long exact sequence for a deletion, with the Gysin map $`g:C(U_{\mathcal{A}''})\to C(U_{\mathcal{A}'})` in it.
It would be natural to look for a reason why $`g` vanishes in the geometry of the divisor.
The normal bundle of the divisor is trivial, since it is cut out by a single function $`f_H`.
But this alone does not prove the vanishing, and the proof uses no property of the normal line at all.

The vanishing is an output of the induction, not an input.
Suppose that the restriction $`\mathcal{A}''` is generated.
Then every class on the divisor is a combination of restricted logarithmic monomials.
Each restricted hyperplane has an old representative, and its logarithmic class restricts to the desired one, because equations differ only by a scalar whose class vanishes.
So every generating monomial lifts to $`U_{\mathcal{A}'}`, and $`i^*` is surjective.
Coefficients lift through the common algebra $`B`, and the lifting extends to sums.

Now the {bp "lem:log-residue"}[residue formula] does the rest.
If $`i^*\beta=z`, then $`[f_H]\,j^*\beta` has residue $`z`, so the residue is surjective.
Since $`\ker g=\operatorname{im}r`, the map $`g` is zero.
Since $`\ker j^*=\operatorname{im}g`, the map $`j^*` is injective.
This is the only place in the proof where the vanishing of Gysin maps is established.

:::node "lem:restriction-surj" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.Generated) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.logMonomial) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.map_complementRestriction_logMonomial_map_representative) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.surjective_map_complementRestriction_of_generated)
If the restriction $`\mathcal{A}''` is generated, then the restriction map $`i^*:C(U_{\mathcal{A}'})\to C(U_{\mathcal{A}''})` is surjective.
:::

:::node "lem:ses" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.IsDeletionShortExact) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.isDeletionShortExact_of_generated)
If the restriction $`\mathcal{A}''` is generated, then the residue is surjective, the Gysin map is zero, and
$$`0\to C(U_{\mathcal{A}'})\xrightarrow{\ j^*\ }C(U_{\mathcal{A}})\xrightarrow{\ r\ }C(U_{\mathcal{A}''})\to0`
is an exact sequence of $`B`-modules, with $`r` of shift $`(-1,-1)`.
:::

# Generation and the bound

The base case is the empty arrangement, whose complement is an affine space, with cohomology $`B` by affine-line invariance.
For the inductive step, choose $`H\in\mathcal{A}`.
Both $`\mathcal{A}'` and $`\mathcal{A}''` have fewer hyperplanes, so both are generated and bounded by induction, and the sequence above is short exact.

For generation, take $`x` in $`C(U_{\mathcal{A}})`.
Its residue is a combination of restricted monomials, which lift as above.
Subtracting $`[f_H]` times the lift gives a class with residue zero, which comes from $`U_{\mathcal{A}'}` and so is generated by old logarithms.

For the bound, pass to components by {bp "lem:extraction"}[homogeneous extraction].
The piece of $`C(U_{\mathcal{A}})` in cohomological degree $`p` sits between the piece of $`C(U_{\mathcal{A}'})` in degree $`p` and the piece of $`C(U_{\mathcal{A}''})` in degree $`p-1`.
If $`p<0` or $`p>n`, then both of these vanish by induction, the second because $`\dim H=n-1`.
Zero-dimensional spaces need no separate argument, since they have no hyperplanes, and their arrangements are the base case.

:::node "thm:generation" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.Generated) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.Bounded) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.generated_and_bounded) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.eq_zero_of_mem_grading_of_finrank_lt) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.eq_zero_of_mem_grading_of_neg) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.isDeletionShortExact)
Every arrangement is generated and bounded, so the short exact sequence above holds for every deletion.
:::

The theorem holds for every arrangement in every finite-dimensional space, with no further condition.
That generality is what {ref "presentation"}[the next chapter] needs: it applies the bound to auxiliary arrangements that have no relation to the arrangement in hand.

# A basis, and why not a rank

The same induction produces a basis.
Keep a basis of $`C(U_{\mathcal{A}'})`, pulled back along $`j^*`.
For each word in a basis of $`C(U_{\mathcal{A}''})`, lift its letters to old hyperplanes and prepend $`H`.
By the residue formula this lifts that basis vector along $`r`, and {bp "lem:basis-ses"}[a short exact sequence] then assembles the two families into a basis of the middle term.
The initial basis is the empty word.

The explicit basis is the useful output, more than a statement about ranks.
The theorem also covers the zero coefficient ring, where every family is a basis, so a numerical rank would not be well defined and would not determine how many basis words have each length.
That count is recorded separately, as part of {name OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.exists_logMonomialBasis}`exists_logMonomialBasis`, because the recursive construction determines it: old words keep their length and lifted words gain one.
{ref "multiplicities"}[The multiplicities chapter] identifies this count.

:::node "lem:basis-ses" (decl := OrlikSolomon.basisOfShortExact) (decl := OrlikSolomon.coe_basisOfShortExact)
In a short exact sequence of modules, a basis of the left term together with lifts of a basis of the right term form a basis of the middle term.
:::

:::node "thm:cohom-basis" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.LogMonomialBasis) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.LogMonomialBasis.basis_mem_grading) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.exists_logMonomialBasis)
For every arrangement, $`C(U_{\mathcal{A}})` has a finite $`B`-basis of products of logarithmic classes along words in the hyperplanes, with each basis vector of bidegree $`(q,q)` for a word of length $`q\le n`.
:::
